# recallwire: the recall draft (runtime::Recall, BLOOMERY_DRAFT=recall) on the seats whose body verifies eight rows
# (Inkling 2 and the qwen3 seat's Body35), W 7 ids a pass; one card, three tables, each its own runner call.
# Tables: (1) Inkling-2-Mini, thinking off; (2) Inkling-2-Mini, thinking on; (3) Qwen3.8-27B (Body35). A table's arms
# are compared only with each other; the two models' rows are never compared (a model term is in every cross-model
# difference), and the two Inkling tables have their own prompts and so their own D and N.
# Decomposition (design.md in /root/agents/specs/recallwire/): a pass verifies 1 + k rows and yields 1 + r tokens, and
# gain = AL / c-bar - 1, c-bar the chooser's mean c(1 + k) steps a pass. The host term (push 1 id + propose, 285-290 ns a
# pass) is 5e-5 of the card's 5.6..6.9 ms step (inkling2-decode.card [derived]), so the wall is the card alone: max = sum,
# no resource sits under another's shadow, and no flow lever (async, bulk, SIMD, SIMT) comes before the wiring.
# AL and steps a pass are the tape's [derived]: ftape/notes.md 8-9 (real greedy tapes of Inkling-2-Mini, the rule's walk).
# c(k) on the 5090 is the one term this run cannot take from a derivation: the A6000 27B column (q35ro, c(2,4,8) = 1.2089 /
# 1.6201 / 2.4007) and Inkling's c(8) = 2.3..3.2 (verify 15.8..18.0 ms against a 5.6..6.9 ms step, derived from
# inkling2-decode.card) are the only cost columns in the repo, and no c(2..7) of Inkling is measured. The width chooser
# (BLOOMERY_MTP_WIDTH unset = cost) takes the width whose AL / c is best from the c it measures, and the arm's
# `mtp width costs=` record prints the c(k) this run is for.
# Tables [derived] (tapescore walk of the a1 tape, off view first 256..1024 tokens and think view first 512..1024):
#   table 1  N = 512, recall vs off +67..+112 % at c(8) 3.2..2.3 (first 256 / 1024 tokens: +57..+127), lookup vs off
#            +32..+46 %, so recall over lookup +27..+45 %; AL 3.1..3.4, c-bar 1.6
#   table 2  N = 1024, recall vs off +78..+111 %, lookup vs off +23..+36 % (the first 512 tokens of a thinking pass are
#            +40..+52 %, the first 256 about +6 %: the window is 1024 so the copy phase is inside it)
#   table 3  N = 512, no tape of this model: the Inkling a1 tape at the A6000 27B column gives +102 % (W <= 7; W <= 3 +85 %),
#            and half of the tape's accepted-length excess gives about +25 %: the model term is what the table prices
# The A6000 columns stand in for the 5090's c(k) in tables 1 and 2 and are the column in table 3 (a different card than
# the 5090 this runs on, the second unknown; the run closes it).
kind: ab
question: does serving the recall draft (BLOOMERY_DRAFT=recall, one slot, the width chooser's pick) raise decode tok/s on an
  edit prompt over the same binary with the draft off, and by how much more than the lookup draft, on Inkling-2-Mini
  (thinking off, thinking on) and on Qwen3.8-27B?
term: decode tok/s (tg, N - 1 decode steps) of the recall arm over the draft-off arm, same binary, paired by round @ n = N
  greedy WITHOUT ignore_eos (a request with ignore_eos takes plain steps under every draft, so all arms would be one program:
  serve_seats/ds41.rs doc, and measured on both real files with /root/agents/specs/recallwire/look.py: draft_n 206..224 of 256 tokens
  without it, none with it; the runner's request needs its EOS mode, /root/agents/specs/recallwire/runner-eos.patch), one slot, bloomery-serve
  at the serving defaults except BLOOMERY_DRAFT, RTX 5090; table 1 Inkling-2-Mini
  UD-Q4_K_XL thinking off, D = 3757, N = 512; table 2 the same file thinking on, D = 3757, N = 1024; table 3 Qwen3.8-27B
  Q4_K_M, D = 3179, N = 512
unit: %
predict: 57..127
rounds: 4
sd: 0.6 % AGENTS.md Know the ruler (same-binary runs scatter with SD 0.6 %)
condition: `predict` is table 1's band; table 2's is +78..+111 and table 3's +25..+102 (each is its own call and the
  table's lines in this header give the derivation). One lease a table, one binary, four arms in one runner call: off,
  lookup, recall and an A/A (the off arm again from a second tree that holds a byte copy of the binary: both trees carry
  the binary and its dep-info after the build, the A/A tree made by the lead's one rsync below, sha256 equal). 16 servers
  a table (4 arms x 4 rounds, one fresh server each), so a table's wall is 16 loads plus 16 short requests; the runner's
  [dry] line prints its predicted wall, and the load seconds of each file are the term this round has not measured.
  The ids files are made, with the tokenizer of each model, from the ftape a1 edit prompt (a rename request over
  crates/runtime/src/lookup.rs and its SEARCH/REPLACE answer format), in /root/agents/specs/recallwire/ids/ (one file a
  prompt and a model, each one id a line, D = its line count):
  ink2-off.ids 3757 ids md5 011d6ec4205b91bcaccfacc073386bf9 (thinking off: the server's apply-template ids with
  enable_thinking false); ink2-think.ids 3757 ids md5 993dbb383c2ebc1ffd1834bf7ed7103c (enable_thinking true); both were
  decoded and re-tokenized by bloomery-tokenize (cargo build --release -p bloomery-tokenizer --bin bloomery-tokenize,
  md5 49e59a9c7a65dc9e68ab0ac7f682da6d) and came back equal id for id:
  bloomery-tokenize -m /models/Inkling-2-Mini-GGUF/Inkling-2-Mini-UD-Q4_K_XL.gguf --decode -f ink2-off.ids > ink2-off.text;
  bloomery-tokenize -m ... -f ink2-off.text --ids. q38-27b.ids 3179 ids md5 721ba3023ba2adf7d872bdf312200fac: the same a1
  user message through the model's ChatML template with thinking off (mk_q38_text.py ids/ftape-a1.prompt.json >
  ids/q38-27b.text, 10692 bytes), bloomery-tokenize -m /models/Qwen3.8-27B/Qwen3.8-27B-Q4_K_M.gguf -f ids/q38-27b.text
  --ids --no-bos, the ids one a line (tr -d '[],' | tr ' ' '\n'); the decode of those ids equals the text byte for byte.
  Only the GGUF headers were read. The binary is the recall tree's bloomery-serve built with the depth-serve recipe's own
  line (cargo oxide build --arch $BLOOMERY_ARCH -- -p bloomery-gpu-gates --features glm5next,clef,mimo2,solar2,minimaxm3,
  flint,kimik3,hy4,ernie55,mistrallarge4,vision,gemma-embedding2,hf,checkpoint --release --bin bloomery-serve, in the tree
  ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire), run once before the first call because the A/A tree is made from its output: rsync -a
  --exclude target --exclude .git ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire/ ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire-aa/ && mkdir -p
  ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire-aa/target/release && cp -a ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire/target/release/bloomery-serve
  ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire/target/release/bloomery-serve.d ~/repo/bloomery-recallwire-aa/target/release/ && cmp the two
  binaries. (A lead's tree of another name replaces the two names in the commands.) Table 1:
  BLOOMERY_MODEL=inkling2 BLOOMERY_AB_ROUNDS=4 BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='DEPTH_SERVE_IDS=/root/agents/specs/recallwire/ids/ink2-off.ids
  DEPTH_SERVE_N=512 LCPP_WARM_EOS=stop BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/recall-edit.card' just depth-serve
  env=BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off:bloomery-recallwire:3757 env=BLOOMERY_DRAFT=lookup:bloomery-recallwire:3757
  env=BLOOMERY_DRAFT=recall:bloomery-recallwire:3757 env=BLOOMERY_DRAFT=off:bloomery-recallwire-aa:3757.
  (LCPP_WARM_EOS=stop is the knob of /root/agents/specs/recallwire/runner-eos.patch, a patch of tools/ref/lcpp-warm.sh the lead applies first;
  without it the request carries ignore_eos and the tables price nothing.) Table 2: the same with DEPTH_SERVE_IDS=/root/agents/specs/recallwire/ids/ink2-think.ids and DEPTH_SERVE_N=1024.
  Table 3: BLOOMERY_MODEL=qwen38d, DEPTH_SERVE_IDS=/root/agents/specs/recallwire/ids/q38-27b.ids, DEPTH_SERVE_N=512 and D
  3179 in each arm.
  Valid only if the runner's request omits ignore_eos and the recall arm's row shows draft_n above 0 (the runner's SRV_DRAFT_N and
  SRV_DRAFT_ACC columns; a recall or lookup arm with draft_n 0 ran plain steps and prices nothing); a response may then end at its
  EOS before N tokens, the same length in every arm because the ids are equal, and the tape bands above are the first-256/512/1024
  token windows of the answer.
  Inside the window of every table: the recall arm's log holds `mtp width` records with `costs=` and `kept=` (the
  chooser's c(k) and the ids kept a pass) and its /props reports draft kind recall n_max 7, the lookup arm's kind lookup
  n_max 1; each arm's response tokens equal the off arm's (the runner prints each arm's request; gate-inkling2-serve and
  gate-qwen3-serve carry the equality clause); no compute process on the 5090 but ours and ComfyUI stopped by the user,
  not by us; the A/A pair lands within 1 % of 1.
decide-in: recall pays on edits as predicted: read the `costs=` row, which width the chooser held and the kept ids a
  pass, then run one more table on a prose prompt (corpus-prose, D 512) off against recall to price the no-match case, and
  propose recall for the unset default only where prose is within the ruler of off; recall over lookup is the number the
  lookup draft is to be compared with (the sitting's gain of the opt-in word, per model)
decide-below: under the table's lo (+57 % Inkling thinking off, +78 % thinking on, +25 % Qwen): read AL and steps a pass
  from the records first: if AL is the tape's and the gain is lower, c(k) is the wrong term, so take c(2..8) of that model
  from the verify rows (gate_inkling2_e2e `verify_walls_ms`) in the same sitting and bound W by the cost column (the
  chooser already does; then the ladder, not the width, is the lever); if AL itself is low, the engine's greedy sequence
  parted from the tape (read the first token that differs) or the edit is less of a copy than the tape's, and recall
  stays opt-in with the copy share (kept ids a pass) as the next number; if it is under +10 % on the Qwen table the
  seat's rule has no use there
decide-above: over the table's hi (+127 %, +111 %, +102 %): the prompt is a purer copy than the tape's or the cost
  column is lower than the one derived; repeat on a prompt whose request rewrites its first lines before it copies and
  read the kept ids a pass before any claim; a gain over the lookup draft that large moves the default question up
